A||||28||loafer87gt||Any recomendations on good reliable car?||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||01-01-2001||11:37 PM||||After long thought, I think I'm going to hold back on repairing my blown motor on my GT until I'm done college. I'm looking for a good reliable car for around the $4000 Canadian price range. I'm looking for something Japanese preferably, any ideas? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||01-02-2001||02:00 AM||||for a good mix of reliability and power, I'd suggest getting something by Oldsmobile.<P>I really don't know much about non-sports car japanese autos though..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||Standard||01-02-2001||04:02 AM||||Japanese and reliable?  Hate to say it, but... Honda Civic.  My girlfriend just got a loaded '90 Honda Civic with 100,000 on it for $4,000 even, and the thing is in perfect shape.  ||||reg||
Z||000003||Wolfhound||01-02-2001||04:19 AM||||Ditto Standard, My wife has a 90 Honda Civic that's just starting to use a little oil.<P>It has 178,000 miles on the odometer!<BR>I,ve only replaced front axle assembles ,timing belt, and muffler.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||01-02-2001||08:33 AM||||I'd have to agree with a Honda car.  They seem to never die.  An Accura (luxury Honda) is another good option.  100,000 miles and they still run strong.  It is just a car, nothing special but highly reliable.||||reg||3
Z||000005||87ECO5||01-02-2001||10:23 AM||||I bet you already know what I'm going to say ...<P>Honda Civic all the way!  I've owned three in my lifetime - all were great!<P>Of course, I could have been different and said Toyota Corolla or Nissan Sentra (had one of these too) ... but when have I ever wanted to be different!?      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Any of these cars will provide high levels of reliability and low levels of "cost of ownership".<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000006||vietnow||01-02-2001||12:28 PM||||my 86 toyota celica GTS has 106k on it and still running strong, same engine and everything.. its very reliable, the only problems they have are usually electrical...(internal not engine)<P>the 86-89 is the fastest and most reliable generation of celicas.. and they dont look that bad either...<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/celimanus/myride.jpg">  <P>oh yeah, that van in the background, is also an 86 toyota, and it has even more miles than my celica and is just as reliable. <BR>------------------<BR>Vietnow --<BR>1986 Celica GTS<p>[This message has been edited by vietnow (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||01-02-2001||01:20 PM||||I was going to say a 95+ Maxima, but then I saw the $4000 statement.. Anyways, IF you were to have more, I would highly suggest that car, as that is probably my favourite car. I haven't had much experience with pre 95 Maximas, but I hear they are quite good and reliable. I suggest you stay away from American cars if you want reliability.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shaft||01-02-2001||01:23 PM||||I have a 90 Nissan Maxima and never had a major problem with it. The only thing expensive that we've had to replace sre the injectors and that's in 10 years of owning it!||||reg||1
Z||000009||p4n1c||01-02-2001||01:59 PM||||IMHO after living in the city and watching all the cars go by, the majority of the ones that I've seen broken down in the middle of lanes and block traffic and show up on the sides of the freeways have been Japanese cars.<P>If you're looking for something small and affordable you could look into a Neon or similiar small car from the Big 3.  Even though Steve recommended Oldsmobile and I agree with him, seeing you're in college, I wouldn't dump the cash in you would need to even buy their entry level car(the Alero), unless you're going used then I would recommend the early W-Body Cutlasses(90-92).<P>Take it easy,<P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||01-02-2001||02:05 PM||||well, i say either a honda civic or accord. my friend has a civic and hasn't had a problem yet that i can think of, except for the battery (his fault) and the timing chain/belt? of course it's a newer model (96) but he's had it for at least 2 years. and i've heard accords are very good cars.<P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||01-02-2001||03:32 PM||||Well, I'm not in love with Hondas.  My buddy here at work bought a 95 Prelude with 66K six months ago - its Check Engine light comes on intermittently and it stalls on him at red lights.  He has had it back for service twice and they can't seem to solve the problem (though they had no problem taking $600 from him).<P>Another work buddy bought a 95 Accord brand new.  It went through its first set of tires in 20K miles - the dealer diagnosed it as misaligned from the factory.  Then the driver's-side power window quit - the dealer replaced it, but it now raises and lowers at half the speed of the others and he has to grab the window and "help" it up and down in subfreezing temps.  Then at 60K miles, the ABS light came on.  Turns out the module was bad - $750 for the repair.<P>My wife bought a one-owner '83 Accord with 63K miles in 1990.  It had a computer-controlled carburetor and never did run quite right - in the summer it ran rough and in the winter it fouled plugs and was damn near impossible to start.  I have never been good with carbs, so we took it to several service shops with no luck.  It was also terribly susceptible to rust - by the time we got rid of it, the mounting points for the rear suspension were rusted so badly as to be hazardous.<P>Some good cheap cars, in my opinion:<P>Toyota Corolla/Geo Prizm<BR>Mazda 323/Protege<BR>Nissan Sentra/Stanza - the boxy kind, not the old rounded-off kind that rusted to the ground<BR>Saturn - old Saturns use oil, but seem to run great, and like Fieros, will never rust<BR>GM sedans: small (Cutlass Calais/ Pontiac Grand Am), medium (Ciera/Celebrity/Century), large (Park Avenue/Bonneville/98 Olds) - the small ones rust, but they all seem to run well<BR>Chevy S-10 pickup trucks<BR>Ford or Chevy full-size pickup trucks, if you can find one beat-up enough to be cheap<BR>Ford Crown Victoria<BR>Chevy Caprice (TBI 305 or 350)<P>Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||loafer87gt||01-02-2001||03:41 PM||||I think I found what I want. A 88-90 Corolla GTS. I did some digging around and found out that these are rear wheel drive!! Sweet! I found a red 89 in the city that I'm gonna go check out.||||reg||1
Z||000013||olympic||01-03-2001||03:40 AM||||My g/f bought a Suzuki Esteem a year ago.  It has 100,000 km and absolutely no problems so far.  It gets 50 mpg hiway and has more than enough power and interior room.  Just be sure to get a 5 speed.  <P>   I put Mobil 1 Synthetic oil in it and it starts even at -35C.  Auto Gallery always has a few in Regina, I'm not sure who the Suzuki dealer is in Saskatoon though.<P>  If your on a tight budget, you cant go wrong with a low mileage 92-94 Tempo/Topaz with the 2.3l and a 5 speed.  I've owned 2 of them and never had any unusual problems.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Motorhead||01-03-2001||10:59 AM||||My 198ZX has 325,000 miles and has only had alternator replaced.  Next project car.<BR>Wifes Geo Prizm (Toyota Corolla in disguise)is at 240,000.  Just replaced the CV joints. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Bigfieroman||01-03-2001||11:27 AM||||Sniff Sniff....I smell Rice!<P>Note: this is not an original joke.  But i got a kick out of it from the original in the clear lights thread.<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Philphine||01-03-2001||01:10 PM||||i've abused an '84 s10 blazer for the last five years (sort of like an experiment in minimal maintenance) and it's still about the most reliable thing i have.||||reg||1
Z||000017||onfire||01-03-2001||04:02 PM||||I own an 86 chevy s10 truck. You should see what it went through last sunday. It still runs and drives great. I have video I might put on the net if anyone is interested.I have got less then $4000 invested in this truck, and it has been one of the most reliable vehicles I have owned.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fformula88||01-03-2001||07:49 PM||||This one may not be what anybody would come up with, but I'd suggest a Ford Probe.  Yes it is Japanese as far as the drivetrain is concerned.  The car is a joint venture between Ford and Mazda, and has a mazda drivetrain.  My first one was a 91 which I never had a major problem with.  I traded it in after 70K miles on my current daily driver, a 97 Probe.  Both cars have been extremely reliable.  An added bonus is they are very sporty (not as much so as a Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).  Also, their resale value has gone way down since Ford discontinued them.  So finding one in excellent condition won't run you an arm and a leg.  Insurance isn't expensive either (for me anyway).  Good luck on whatever you find!  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/97proberiver1.JPG"> <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000019||Cooter||01-03-2001||09:46 PM||||I have a '90 Civic Si that I drive the crap out of. It has been totaled twice- I bought it wrecked and then it was smashed in a parking lot accident. Without a doubt, it has been the best car I have ever had. 150,000+ miles and so far only one axle (replaced free by dealer), one clutch, timing belt and alternator. Oh, and I am on my second set of tires and rear brake shoes. It has always used a quart of oil between changes (about 7,000 miles-Mobil 1) but still gets well over 35 MPG on the road and 30 in town.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Raydar||01-03-2001||11:19 PM||||Let me add another recommendation for the S-10 or Sonoma.<BR>I bought my Sonoma new in 92. 4.3 5-speed. It has 97K on it now. Have had to replace one alternator, and the AC compressor locked up last summer. And have broken two left side motor mounts, but that's not the truck's fault.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>Have replaced front brake pads twice. <BR>Other than that, nada.<BR>D@mn thing's nearly bulletproof.<P>Raydar ||||reg||1
Z||000021||stsmithgt||01-04-2001||01:51 PM||||Wow, all these posts for Honda. Hmmm, if I get a Honda, I could save money so I could sink some more into my GT in the long run.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000022||Motorhead||01-04-2001||04:56 PM||||Fformula- My wife who is an EX-CAR SALES"PERSON"/MANAGER says--"Probe body panels are attached with two sided tape"!  PLEASE CLARIFY!!!<BR>She sold the following during her tenure.<BR>Toyota,Ford,Chrysler(HA HA),and Pontiac.<BR>"CAR SALES PERSON"-Contradiction in terms HUH!<BR>SAVING GRACE: She wants her own FIERO!!!!<BR>PEACE!! Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fformula88||01-04-2001||06:47 PM||||Two sided tape?  Maybe I am being super dense here but I am not even sure what its trying to make fun of.  The structural integrity of the car is very good.  There is a little body flex ogoing around tight corners from the hatchback design though.  But a decent strut brace will clear that right up.  I have seen very few Probe's with any kind of serious rust, even the first 1989's.  They are good in crash tests (I think 5 star driver, 4 passenger). <P>Maybe your wife had gotten in a wrecked Probe which was put back together with two-sided tape!  Of course, I find it very funny someone who wants a Fiero would make fun of the Probe's construction  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Those plastic body panels might as well be taped on!  The plastic panels could be taped on!!!  j/k Motorhead.  But it seems to be a very well built car to me.  Better than my mom's Buick or grandfather's Dakota (another Chrysler product!  hahaha).||||reg||1
Z||000024||2xsess||01-04-2001||09:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>Well, I'm not in love with Hondas.  My buddy here at work bought a 95 Prelude with 66K six months ago - its Check Engine light comes on intermittently and it stalls on him at red lights.  He has had it back for service twice and they can't seem to solve the problem (though they had no problem taking $600 from him).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had a similar problem with my 92 Prelude, it turned out to be the distributor, there' a part in there that gets worn out. Mine finally got so bad it actually would grind around inside the distributor. $250 later it ran like a dream again. I think it had 140,000 on it at that point.<BR>The timing belt broke at 106,000 miles, then $1600 later it ran but always had an oil leak, not Hondas fault.<P>I also had to replace the battery and the alternator, I bought it with 54,000 miles on it. Of course I have no idea what the cars history was.<P>I had a 1990 Toyota Celica that I put 9,000 miles on in 6 months and never had a problem withit until I found a nice shiny 86Fiero GT for sale  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> that was the end of my ownership of the Celica.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Motorhead||01-05-2001||11:59 AM||||Sorry Fformula, not "dis"ing the car! Seems every time we take the Fiero out a Probe wants to race.  Weird!<BR>She had a Probe as a demo for awhile.  I liked the car.  Nice for what it was.<BR>She also drove an RX7 as a demo and I really enjoyed driving it.  Wankel engine and all.<BR>All time favorite has to be the Bonneville SSEI though. WHOA!! Ultimate "old fart" performance sedan. <BR>And the 6-speed Firebird Firehawk. Only drove it once, but VERY NICE MACHINE!!!  <BR>But nothing HANDLES like our beloved Fieros!!<BR>Peace<BR>Mike<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||Fformula88||01-05-2001||07:10 PM||||Hey, no offense taken Motorhead!  I thought it was very funny  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">!  I get a lot of Probes who want to race my Fiero too.  I think a lot of punk kids own them, like Honda's.  You should see some of the bs that goes on in the Probe forum!  LOL, its funny though.  <P>I agree with the Bonneville's!  My friend has one (I think its a 92?).  Its huge, but has a ton of pickup and is overall a very nice car.  Not me though.  My favorite car is my uncles 79 Trans Am.  He has rebuilt the engine (originally a 402) to a 454 with performance cam.  Its SOOO much fun to light 'em up with the posi rear end!  Maybe I am in the minority, but I love the big red screaming chicken on the hood too!  Really stands out on his car because it is yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000027||Motorhead||01-06-2001||11:27 AM||||The gospel according to the wife is that the ground effects on the Probe "GT" were applied with two sided tape.  She sold them so I guess she knows.  But then if you drive a Fiero onto a Pontiac lot today, most of the salesman wouldn't know that it was a Pontiac product.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Fformula88||01-06-2001||02:37 PM||||Speaking of a Fiero on a Pontiac lot... I was looking at a Firebird when I bought the Probe a couple years back.  The salesman was very proud of the plastic doors and front fenders and was giving me all the "advantages".  Basically he was pounding them to show how dent resistant they were.  Well, I replied how much I do like the panels since my Fiero was made of them.  Pointing in its direction out in the lot, I said how great it is to have a car that was 11 years old (at the time) and not have a spot of rust on it!  The salesman was dumbfounded.  He had no idea the Fiero had plastic panels.  I also mentioned the rear engine layout which he also did not know about.  I think he looked at my Fiero as much as I was looking at the Firebird!  He didn't get a test drive  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>Oh, just another sidebar on the Probe GFX (as if this is really important).  They are attached with little plastic "bottons" I guess you'd call them.  My friend added them to his car which didn't come with them on.  He bought them from ford... a total of 76 for both sides and drilled as many holes into the side of his car.  Amazingly he got them in the right spots and the finished product turned out great!  But I wouldn't do it 'cause its just asking for big old rust holes in a few years... especially in Buffalo!  Sounds like they probably had tape in there too.  It would make sense since you wouldn't want them to rattle, and those clips wouldn't hold it down tight everywhere.||||reg||1
